WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.530
In the seventh episode of Star Trek: Picard,
the characters split up as Elnor remains on

00:00:04.530 --> 00:00:09.040
the Borg Artifact, Picard and Soji go visit
old friends, and the rest of the La Sirena

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:09.900
crew heads after him.

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:13.420
Here are all the Easter eggs and references
you might have missed in "Nepenthe."

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:17.751
When Picard and Soji are confronted in the
woods of Nepenthe by Will Riker and Deanna

00:00:17.751 --> 00:00:21.830
Troi's daughter, Kestra, she points her bow
and arrow at Picard's heart but he tells her

00:00:21.830 --> 00:00:26.160
she may want to aim at his head instead, since
his heart is made of solid duritanium.

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:31.180
Picard's artificial heart was first established
as canon in the second season episode, "Samaritan

00:00:31.189 --> 00:00:35.540
Snare," in which Picard has to undergo an
operation to replace the deteriorating mechanical

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:36.040
organ.

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:39.980
Picard says his original heart replacement
occurred after being stabbed through the back

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:45.370
by a group of Naussicans when he was younger,
which he tells Wesley was a curious sensation.

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:46.780
"Not much pain.

00:00:47.660 --> 00:00:54.320
Shock, certainly, at the sight of the serrated
metal sticking through my chest."

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:58.560
Later, in the sixth season episode "Tapestry,"
we actually see this confrontation play out

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:00.360
thanks to some time meddling from Q.

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:05.040
When Picard visits Will Riker and Deanna Troi,
we learn that the couple has had two children

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:07.400
in the time that has passed since Star Trek:
Nemesis.

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:12.490
Their first, a boy named Thad, would've been
eighteen in 2399, but tragically passed away

00:01:12.490 --> 00:01:13.640
several years earlier.

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:17.700
At the time of "Nepenthe," they only have
one surviving child, a precocious young teen

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:19.760
with a vivid imagination named Kestra.

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:24.400
Both of the Troi-Riker children were actually
named for members of their parents' families.

00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:28.320
As revealed in the Voyager episode "Death
Wish," Thaddeus Riker was a soldier in the

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:32.810
Union Army during the American Civil War whose
life was saved by a Q named Quinn.

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:37.639
"That is Colonel Thadeius Riker after he was
wounded at Pine Mountain.

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:39.399
They used to call him 'Old Iron Boots.'"

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:43.759
But while Thad Troi-Riker was named for a
distant ancestor of his father, Kestra got

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:46.080
her name from a much closer relation to her
mother.

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:51.030
As revealed in the Next Generation episode,
"Dark Page," Kestra Troi was Deanna's older

00:01:51.030 --> 00:01:53.560
sister, who died as a child in a tragic accident.

00:01:53.889 --> 00:01:57.340
Deanna didn't know about Kestra until she
was an adult, when she helped her mother finally

00:01:57.340 --> 00:02:00.360
work through her decades-old grief and guilt
over Kestra's death.

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:04.719
In Riker's first scene in Picard, he's working
in the kitchen, preparing ingredients for

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:06.590
homemade pizza while listening to jazz.

00:02:06.940 --> 00:02:10.940
Both of these pastimes are things Picard's
erstwhile Number One often enjoyed in between

00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:12.060
shifts on the Enterprise.

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:16.480
In the Next Generation season 2 episode "Time
Squared," Riker makes scrambled eggs from

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:21.320
scratch for his fellow crewmates, using Owan
eggs he picked up on their last stop at Starbase

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:21.900
73.

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:26.500
Unfortunately, it turns out that Owan eggs
aren't appetizing to humans, and most of Riker's

00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:28.500
guests can't manage more than a mouthful.

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:29.780
In his defense, Riker says,

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:32.240
"A cook is only as good as his ingredients."

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:35.900
In "Nepenthe," Riker seems to have learned
his lesson, using tomatoes and basil from

00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:37.300
his own garden in his pizza.

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:41.300
Meanwhile, Will Riker's love for jazz was
first shown in the Next Generation season

00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:44.620
1 episode, "11001001."

00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:49.200
In that episode, an alien species called the
Bynars distracts Riker by keeping him on the

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:53.080
holodeck, where he plays trombone with a 1958
New Orleans jazz combo.

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:57.879
After learning that Soji is a synthetic modeled
after Commander Data, Kestra begins quizzing

00:02:57.880 --> 00:02:59.909
her new acquaintance on her interests and
abilities.

00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:01.800
"Can you play the violin?"

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:03.540
No."

00:03:03.860 --> 00:03:05.060
"Do you like Sherlock Holmes?"

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:07.460
"I guess."

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:11.680
Data first picked up the violin in order to
get into character as Sherlock Holmes in the

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:14.780
Next Generation season 2 episode, "Elementary,
Dear Data."

00:03:15.129 --> 00:03:19.980
In the episode, Data and Geordi are confronted
with a hologram of Holmes' arch-nemesis, Moriarty,

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:21.709
who begins to gain sentience.

00:03:21.709 --> 00:03:26.260
The themes of "Elementary, Dear Data," about
artificial intelligence which becomes self-aware,

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:28.000
are very similar to those of Picard.

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:32.640
Kestra also asks whether Soji has any enhanced
physical abilities, like bending metal with

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:33.520
her bare hands.

00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:37.780
On The Next Generation, this was one of the
demonstrations that Riker himself uses to

00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:40.980
argue against Data's humanity in the episode,
"The Measure of a Man."

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:45.640
Fortunately, despite Riker presenting a strong
case on behalf of Bruce Maddox, the judge

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:47.670
rules in Data and Picard's favor.

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:54.720
"It is the ruling of this court that Lieutenant
Commander Data has the freedom to choose."

00:03:54.730 --> 00:03:59.500
Riker later apologizes to Data for arguing
that he wasn't a person, but Data immediately

00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:03.020
absolves him, telling him that if he hadn't
done it, Data never would've been given the

00:04:03.020 --> 00:04:05.320
opportunity to defend himself in the first
place.

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:09.880
After quizzing Soji on whether she inherited
any of her interests and abilities from her

00:04:09.890 --> 00:04:14.069
"father," Data, Kestra moves on to her biological
functions, wondering if her synthetic body

00:04:14.069 --> 00:04:16.140
produces things like saliva and mucus.

00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:20.380
When Soji confirms that it does, Kestra muses
that it would make sense that Data's synthetic

00:04:20.389 --> 00:04:24.669
offspring would have so many of these biological
traits, since he was always trying to be more

00:04:24.669 --> 00:04:25.160
human.

00:04:25.669 --> 00:04:29.660
She goes on to explain that despite all of
Data's impressive android abilities, all he

00:04:29.660 --> 00:04:33.181
ever really wanted to do were things like
have dreams, tell jokes, and learn how to

00:04:33.181 --> 00:04:34.040
ballroom dance.

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:38.080
Data was featured doing all three of these
activities during various episodes of Star

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:39.280
Trek: The Next Generation.

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:43.280
Data first started experiencing dreams in
the sixth season episode, "Birthright, Part

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:43.780
I."

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:47.980
After that episode, he said that he planned
to incorporate dreaming into his regular routine.

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:52.550
He also repeatedly tried to tell jokes throughout
The Next Generation's run, and even received

00:04:52.550 --> 00:04:56.919
lessons in humor from a holographic standup
comedian in the season 2 episode, "The Outrageous

00:04:56.920 --> 00:04:57.520
Okona."

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:02.520
"I come from a town so small, we had a fraction
for a zip code."

00:05:05.020 --> 00:05:09.700
And he hilariously asks for dancing lessons
from Dr. Crusher in the season 4 episode "Data's

00:05:09.700 --> 00:05:12.120
Day," which also features the return of Bruce
Maddox.

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:16.540
Due to a miscommunication, Crusher teaches
Data to tap dance, when what he really needed

00:05:16.540 --> 00:05:19.020
to learn was ballroom dancing for an upcoming
wedding.

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:23.820
While attempting to persuade Rios and Raffi
to abandon Picard and return to Earth, Agnes

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:26.660
says she'd hoped to be the fun crew member
who says things like,

00:05:26.980 --> 00:05:30.460
"'Let's hide in that comet' and it turns out
to be a giant gormagander or something"

00:05:30.460 --> 00:05:35.200
Gormaganders resemble giant space whales and
were first seen in the Star Trek: Discovery

00:05:35.210 --> 00:05:39.510
episode, "Magic to Make the Sanest Man Go
Mad," in which a character hid inside an injured

00:05:39.510 --> 00:05:43.440
gormagander in an attempt to smuggle himself
on board the Discovery and take over the ship.

00:05:43.910 --> 00:05:48.220
But of course, hiding in a comet that turns
out to be a giant organic lifeform is also

00:05:48.220 --> 00:05:51.280
a clear reference to that other hugely popular
space saga.

00:05:51.660 --> 00:05:55.960
In The Empire Strikes Back, Han Solo hides
from the Imperial Navy by piloting the Millenium

00:05:55.960 --> 00:06:00.460
Falcon into a cave in an asteroid, which turns
out to actually be the mouth of a giant space

00:06:00.460 --> 00:06:00.960
slug.

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:05.120
When trying to convince the adults around
the dinner table that her friend Captain Crandall

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:09.590
would be the ideal candidate to transport
Picard and Soji off-world on his ship, Kestra

00:06:09.590 --> 00:06:11.520
brags about Crandall's travels, saying,

00:06:11.660 --> 00:06:13.680
"He's been everywhere from Qo'noS to Tyken's
Rift."

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:17.870
Qo'noS has been mentioned many times throughout
Star Trek history as the home of the Klingon

00:06:17.870 --> 00:06:21.639
Empire, and Picard and his crew have even
visited it a few times.

00:06:21.639 --> 00:06:26.370
Meanwhile, Tyken's Rift isn't a planet, but
rather a type of spatial anomaly that absorbs

00:06:26.370 --> 00:06:27.720
all the surrounding energy.

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:32.140
The Enterprise became trapped in a Tyken's
Rift in the Next Generation season 4 episode,

00:06:32.140 --> 00:06:32.760
"Night Terrors."

00:06:33.150 --> 00:06:36.980
In that episode, Counselor Troi was the one
who worked out the solution so that the Enterprise

00:06:36.980 --> 00:06:38.640
could finally break free of the Rift.

00:06:39.020 --> 00:06:43.300
Years later, we see that Picard is still seeking
advice from Troi to get himself out of seemingly

00:06:43.300 --> 00:06:47.060
impossible situations, and that she is still
more than willing to give it.

